color-theme: genetics

=== layout: two-panels

# All Four Products Stay Together

@left

- In contrast, some fungi [*maintain all four haploid products of meiosis in a single sac called an ascus*]{color=#ba00ba}.
- These haploid cells survive as viable individuals that grow and perpetuate themselves by mitosis.
  - The phenotype of such haploid fungi is a direct representation of their genotype, without complications of dominance.

@right

![One ascus contains four labeled haploid spores](assets/exemplar_03_figures_comparisons/homework_ascus.drawn.svg)

@citation

Homework symbols: lowercase letters = mutant alleles; + = wild type. Colors identify alleles.

=== layout: two-panels

# Why Keep the Four Products Together?

@left

![The homework ascus with its sac and an ascospore labeled](assets/exemplar_03_figures_comparisons/homework_ascus.drawn.svg)

@right

- Each ascus keeps the four products of a single meiosis together.
- Each haploid spore can grow by mitosis.
- Its phenotype directly reveals its genotype; dominance does not hide the alleles.
- In unordered tetrads, the positions of the four spores do not preserve meiotic order.

@citation

The colored sectors represent alleles in the homework; they are not actual spore pigmentation.

@notes

The yeast mating types a and alpha describe mating compatibility. They are separate from the mutant allele symbols used in the mapping problems.
Point to each of the four spores. The three characters within one spore are three loci in one haploid genotype. Two copies of each allele occur across these four spores; their positions do not identify the meiotic divisions.

=== layout: two-panels

# Two Copies of the Same Allele

@left

- Before the exchange, the cell is *A/a*.
- One way of separating the chromosomes gives *A/A* and *a/a* daughters.
- Each daughter still has two copies of the chromosome.
- Two copies, but only one allele version: *loss of heterozygosity (LOH)*.

@right

![Two diploid daughters become homozygous at a distal marker](assets/exemplar_03_figures_comparisons/loh_outcome.drawn.svg)

@citation

Hagstrom and Dryja (1999): https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.96.6.2952

@notes

Name the genotype change shown here. This diagram follows one gene. It omits replication and the four-chromatid intermediate. The exchange is between the centromere and A, followed by the segregation that produces A/A and a/a daughters. The alternate segregation pattern can leave both daughters A/a. Loss of heterozygosity can also happen by other mechanisms.

=== layout: two-panels

# One Event Can Make a Patch

@left

- Each daughter can divide to make a patch.
- Cells from one starting cell form a *clone*.
- An individual with cells of different genotypes is a *mosaic*.
- An earlier exchange often gives a larger patch.
- Cell growth and survival also affect patch size.

@right

![Two daughter lineages produce neighboring clones](assets/exemplar_03_figures_comparisons/clonal_patches.drawn.svg)

@citation

Graf (1995): https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01929364

=== layout: image-comparison

# From Daughter Cells to Patches

@top-left

![Two diploid daughters become homozygous at a distal marker](assets/exemplar_03_figures_comparisons/loh_outcome.drawn.svg)

@top-right

![Two daughter lineages produce neighboring clones](assets/exemplar_03_figures_comparisons/clonal_patches.drawn.svg)

@bottom-left

*A/A* and *a/a*: two copies, one allele version.

@bottom-right

Each daughter can grow into a *clone*.

=== layout: one-panel

# What Is the Gene Order?

@body

![Observed recombination: CD 30%, CH 20%, DH 40%, shown on the same percentage scale.](assets/exemplar_03_figures_comparisons/pairwise_results.drawn.svg)

Which pair has the largest recombination frequency?

=== layout: one-panel

# Three Choices, One Middle Gene

@body

{table-style=standard}
| Middle Gene | Gene Order | Same Map Reversed |
| --- | --- | --- |
| C | D - *C* - H | H - *C* - D |
| D | C - *D* - H | H - *D* - C |
| H | C - *H* - D | D - *H* - C |

Reversing a map gives the same order: only *three choices*.

D-H has the largest recombination frequency. If D and H are the ends,
*which gene is in the middle?*

=== layout: big-image

@image

![D-C-H is the gene order; H-C-D is equivalent.](assets/exemplar_03_figures_comparisons/gene_order_middle.drawn.svg)

@caption

D-C-H and H-C-D describe the same gene order.

=== layout: one-panel

# Why Don't the Results Add?

@body

![A proportional map: D to C is 30 cM, C to H is 20 cM; the sum is 50 cM while D-H observed recombination is 40%.](assets/exemplar_03_figures_comparisons/gene_map.drawn.svg)

Which offspring count for both adjacent intervals, but not for D-H?

=== layout: one-panel

# Count Both Adjacent Intervals

@body

{column-widths="2.5,1,1,1,1" padding="1pt 4pt" table-style=testcross}
| Original Label | Count | [CD]{color=#182033 background-color=#FF8F8F text-align=center} | [CH]{color=#182033 background-color=#8FABFF text-align=center} | [DH]{color=#182033 background-color=#DC9AF4 text-align=center} |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| cdh | 29 | [R]{background-color=#FF8F8F text-align=center} | [R]{background-color=#8FABFF text-align=center} | [P]{background-color=#D6FFAD text-align=center} |
| +++ | 21 | [R]{background-color=#FF8F8F text-align=center} | [R]{background-color=#8FABFF text-align=center} | [P]{background-color=#D6FFAD text-align=center} |

50 / 1,000 &times; 100 = *5%* double recombinants.

These 50 offspring are *already counted in both intervals*: 100 interval counts.

[30]{color=#D40000} + [20]{color=#003FFF} = *50 cM*

[40]{color=#A719DB} + 2 &times; 5 = *50 cM*

@citation

R = recombinant; P = parental for the indicated pair. Original labels use C-D-H order.
